# --- Auth: use your Claude subscription, not a metered API key ---
# Generate once with:  claude setup-token
# Then paste the token here. This is what makes the agent "free" on your Max plan.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=

# --- Who may talk to the agent (comma-separated, E.164). Inbound from anyone else is ignored. ---
OWNER_NUMBERS=+1XXXXXXXXXX

# Your email handles (comma-separated). iMessage sometimes delivers your texts from an
# Apple-ID email instead of the number — list those addresses so they still count as you.
# Also the default Find My poll handle (location sharing rides the Apple ID, not the number).
OWNER_EMAILS=

# Your name, used in the system prompt — what the agent calls you.
OWNER_NAME=

# --- Identity (rename in one place) ---
# The agent's name. Leave unset to use DEFAULT_AGENT_NAME in src/core/config.ts.
AGENT_NAME=

# --- Storage ---
# The vault / "brain". Defaults to ../brain (a sibling git repo). ~ is expanded.
BRAIN_DIR=
# Extra directories the agent may write to without asking (comma-separated). e.g. project repos.
EXTRA_WRITABLE_DIRS=

# --- Model ---
AGENT_MODEL=claude-opus-4-8
# Email triage follows /model's provider but always uses that provider's light tier
# (this model for Claude; gpt-5.6-luna/low for Codex).
EMAIL_TRIAGE_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5
# Kill switch: force all email triage back through Claude regardless of /model.
# TRIAGE_RUNTIME=claude

# --- Warm session (persistent streaming session) ---
# WARM_SESSION=1 runs each turn on ONE long-lived streaming session that keeps the MCP
# fleet (calendar/email/browse/…) connected ACROSS turns, instead of cold-booting the
# whole fleet per message — a cold boot per message shows up as mid-convo "tool dropped"
# blips. Pre-warms the CLI subprocess + MCP handshake at boot. Falls back to the cold one-shot
# path on any warm trouble that delivered nothing, so a message is never dropped.
# KILL-SWITCH: unset or WARM_SESSION=0 → instant revert to the exact cold path.
WARM_SESSION=1

# --- Context budget (how much conversation rides in the model each turn) ---
# Resuming an SDK session replays its WHOLE transcript every turn, so an active
# day grows unbounded. Once the live context crosses this many tokens, the bot
# rolls over to a fresh session reseeded from the log below (the dropped tail stays
# grep-able under Conversations/). Lower = cheaper, less in-context history.
# SESSION_MAX_CONTEXT_TOKENS=150000
# Fresh-session seed (the combo): keep >= FLOOR most-recent messages, plus all
# messages within the last WINDOW_H hours, capped at CEIL.
# SESSION_SEED_FLOOR=30
# SESSION_SEED_WINDOW_H=12
# SESSION_SEED_CEIL=120
#
# "Pack our own bags" rollover compaction (OFF by default). When on, a rollover runs
# one selected-model extraction pass and carries a structured working-state block (open task,
# decisions+why, exact file index, verbatim recent intent) into the fresh session's
# seed, persisted to .state/working-state.json so it survives many rollovers. This is
# what stops a rollover from forgetting where code lives / what was decided.
# SESSION_WORKING_STATE=1
# WORKING_STATE_TIMEOUT_MS=45000
# WORKING_STATE_MAX_DECISIONS=60
# WORKING_STATE_MAX_FILES=80
# WORKING_STATE_INTENT_CHARS=8000
# WORKING_STATE_TTL_H=24                  # drop accumulated state older than this

# --- Transport ---
# Comma list; first = primary (where proactive messages go). Channels: relay
# (HTTP relay — handles inbound+outbound iMessage), imsg (local BlueBubbles),
# telegram (no Apple dependency — the easiest way to get running).
# Start here: TRANSPORT=telegram. Add imsg once the Mac lanes are set up.
TRANSPORT=telegram
POLL_INTERVAL_MS=4000

# --- Telegram (the zero-setup channel — TRANSPORT=telegram) ---
# No Mac/iCloud/phone-plan dependency; always reachable, and the fastest first run.
# Get a token from @BotFather, text the bot once, then set the chat id it logs.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
TELEGRAM_OWNER_CHAT_ID=

# --- HTTP iMessage relay (set TRANSPORT=relay to use it) ---
# Bridges to a separate BlueBubbles-backed relay service. Inbound is PUSH: the relay
# POSTs new texts to our webhook, so the bot runs a tiny in-process listener. Run the bot
# ON the same Mac as the relay and it's all localhost — no tunnel, nothing exposed.
#   RELAY_SECRET must equal the webhook secret configured on the relay, and the relay's
#   webhook URL must point at the host/port/path below.
RELAY_URL=http://localhost:8080
RELAY_SECRET=
RELAY_WEBHOOK_PORT=8787
RELAY_WEBHOOK_HOST=127.0.0.1
RELAY_WEBHOOK_PATH=/inbound
# With push delivery you can poll the in-memory buffer faster for snappier replies:
# POLL_INTERVAL_MS=1000

# Apple Health ingest — the "Health Auto Export" iOS app (REST API export) POSTs
# health JSON here over Tailscale. Bind 0.0.0.0 so the Tailscale interface is
# reachable; the Bearer secret is the auth gate. Disabled if HEALTH_SECRET is empty.
HEALTH_WEBHOOK_HOST=0.0.0.0
HEALTH_WEBHOOK_PORT=8788
HEALTH_WEBHOOK_PATH=/health
HEALTH_SECRET=

# --- Quiet hours for proactive messages (24h local) ---
QUIET_START=23
QUIET_END=8

# --- Agent credential injector (Bitwarden CLI) ---
# Used only inside the browser specialist. `bw get item ...` runs inside the
# credential tool, verifies the active browser origin against config/credential-handles.json,
# fills the focused login form, and never returns plaintext to the model.
# Install once: `brew install bitwarden-cli`
#
# Recommended (robust, survives restarts): let the daemon self-unlock from env.
# Use a SEPARATE Bitwarden account that holds ONLY the few creds you allow.
#   1. In that account: Settings → Security → Keys → "View API Key" → copy
#      client_id + client_secret into BW_CLIENTID / BW_CLIENTSECRET below.
#   2. Put that account's master password in BW_PASSWORD.
# The injector runs `bw login --apikey` + `bw unlock` itself; nothing enters the model.
BW_CLIENTID=
BW_CLIENTSECRET=
BW_PASSWORD=
#
# Escape hatch (alternative to the above): paste a one-off unlocked session.
# Goes stale on restart, so the env-vars path above is preferred for the daemon.
#   export BW_SESSION="$(bw unlock --raw)"
BW_SESSION=

# --- Spotify (playlist skill) — OAuth, same shape as Google above ---
# NOT a bearer/access token (those expire in ~1h). You need a developer app +
# a refresh token, which the agent trades for fresh access tokens at runtime.
#   1. Create an app at https://developer.spotify.com/dashboard → copy Client ID + Secret.
#   2. Add a Redirect URI on the app (must match SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URL exactly).
#   3. Mint the refresh token once:  npm run auth:spotify   (writes it here for you).
# Scopes needed to create playlists: playlist-modify-private playlist-modify-public
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_ID=
SPOTIFY_CLIENT_SECRET=
SPOTIFY_REDIRECT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8888/callback
SPOTIFY_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# --- Last.fm (playlist skill — taste grounding, read-only) ---
# Free API key: https://www.last.fm/api/account/create  (only the key + your username).
LASTFM_API_KEY=
LASTFM_USERNAME=

# --- Google accounts (multi-account Gmail + Calendar) ---
# Default: one implicit "primary" account using GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN (single-account,
# nothing to set here). To add more, list labels and mint a token per account:
#   1. GOOGLE_ACCOUNTS=primary,school
#   2. npm run auth:google school   → signs into that account, writes GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN_SCHOOL
# Search + triage + calendar then cover ALL accounts automatically; the agent never has
# to be told which inbox/calendar a message or event is in, and you never specify one.
# GOOGLE_ACCOUNTS=primary,school
# GOOGLE_REFRESH_TOKEN_SCHOOL=

# --- Gmail push (Pub/Sub PULL) + calendar change sync ---
# Email triage is event-driven via Gmail watch → Pub/Sub, pulled from our own
# subscription (no public endpoint). Needs the `pubsub` OAuth scope, so after adding
# this, re-mint the token: npm run auth:google. Create a topic (`gmail-push`) and a pull
# subscription (`bot-gmail-pull`) in your own GCP project, then put its id here — the
# topic/subscription lines below only need editing if you named them something else.
GCP_PROJECT_ID=your-gcp-project-id
GMAIL_PUBSUB_TOPIC=projects/your-gcp-project-id/topics/gmail-push
GMAIL_PUBSUB_SUBSCRIPTION=projects/your-gcp-project-id/subscriptions/bot-gmail-pull
# GMAIL_PULL_INTERVAL_MS=5000      # floor between pull cycles
# Calendar changes use incremental sync (no webhook). Poll cadence + which calendar.
# CAL_POLL_MS=60000
# CAL_CALENDAR_ID=primary

# --- Non-Gmail mail accounts (poll-based triage; DARK until flipped on) ---
# The inboxes with no API path. One poll loop per account, each with its own state file.
OUTLOOK_POLL=1                     # enable the mail poller (absent/anything-else = off)
# Which accounts to drive: key|identity|human label|transport, records separated by `;`.
# The FIRST one is the default for any tool call that names no account. The key is short
# and stable — it's what the agent passes as `account`, and it names the poll-state file
# (.state/mail-poll-<key>.json).
#   identity  = the EXACT Mail.app account name (applemail) or the email address (imap)
#   transport = applemail (default, omit it) | imap | imap:ENVPREFIX
# MAIL_ACCOUNTS=outlook|Exchange|School Outlook (school); personal|you@example.com|you@example.com (personal)|imap:PRIVATEEMAIL
#
# An `imap` account talks to the server directly (IMAP read/write-back + SMTP for a
# confirmed send) instead of going through Mail.app — which is not a preference: on a headless
# Mac, Mail.app cannot have an account added at all. ENVPREFIX (default:
# the key, uppercased) names these:
# PRIVATEEMAIL_ADDRESS=you@example.com           # full address = the IMAP/SMTP username
# PRIVATEEMAIL_PASSWORD=                         # mailbox password (this file is 600 + gitignored)
# PRIVATEEMAIL_IMAP_HOST=mail.privateemail.com
# PRIVATEEMAIL_IMAP_PORT=993        # implicit TLS
# PRIVATEEMAIL_SMTP_HOST=           # defaults to the IMAP host
# PRIVATEEMAIL_SMTP_PORT=465        # implicit TLS
# PRIVATEEMAIL_WEBMAIL_URL=         # defaults to https://<imap host>/ — IMAP has no per-message link
#
# Apple-Mail-only form, still honored when MAIL_ACCOUNTS is unset (every record applemail):
# APPLE_MAIL_ACCOUNTS=outlook|Exchange|School Outlook (school)
# Legacy single-account form, honored when both of the above are unset (key `outlook`):
# OUTLOOK_MAIL_ACCOUNT=Exchange
# OUTLOOK_POLL_INTERVAL_MS=90000    # poll cadence
# OUTLOOK_POLL_SCAN=25              # inbox heads per fetch page
# OUTLOOK_POLL_SCAN_MAX=200         # max heads swept per tick / baseline seed size
#
# smoke-test first (read-only, all accounts): npm run outlook:test
# and for a freshly added account:            npm run outlook:test -- --ensure-folders

# --- Outbound voice calls (Vapi) ---
# Lets the agent call a business on your behalf (confirm hours, check an order, sit on hold).
# All set up via the Vapi CLI/REST — no dashboard needed.
#   1. Install + login:  curl -sSL https://vapi.ai/install.sh | bash  &&  vapi login
#   2. API key is in ~/.vapi-cli.yaml after login (the `api_key:` field).
#   3. Get a free US number + the assistant id from `vapi phone list` / `vapi assistant list`.
VAPI_API_KEY=
VAPI_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
VAPI_ASSISTANT_ID=

# --- Image generation (OpenAI gpt-image-2) ---
# Get a key at platform.openai.com → API keys.
# gpt-image-2: low ~$0.006/img, medium ~$0.05, high ~$0.21
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- AI-text detection (Pangram) ---
# Powers mcp__pangram__detect — "is this AI-written?" on pasted text.
#   1. Get a key at https://www.pangram.com (dashboard → API).
#   2. Paste it below. Billing is ~$0.05 / 1,000 words; a $25 credit pack lasts a long time.
# NOTE: text sent to this API LEAVES the machine. Never feed it private work/NDA material.
PANGRAM_API_KEY=
# Optional model selector from `GET /models`. Unset = pangram-4, pinned deliberately: the
# API's own "default" selector can resolve to an older model that emits neither the AI-Assisted
# window label nor the humanizer head the tool's verdict and caveats are built on.
# PANGRAM_MODEL=default

# --- fig's own email (AgentMail) ---
# Gives fig its OWN email, separate from your inbox. It uses this to sign up for
# accounts/sites with burner addresses while browsing — it creates an inbox, enters the
# address, and reads the verification code or magic link back on its own — and for
# anything else it needs an email of its own for. Sending outbound mail still asks you.
#   1. Sign up at https://agentmail.to and create an API key (starts with am_).
#   2. Paste it below. Inboxes are created on demand at runtime — nothing else to set up.
AGENTMAIL_API_KEY=
# fig's persistent address — the inbox it knows as "its own" (surfaced in its prompt, and
# the default for the read-only inbox tools). Create it once at agentmail.to, then paste it
# here. Burner signups still mint fresh throwaway inboxes; this is just the stable one.
# AGENTMAIL_ADDRESS=figbot@agentmail.to
# Optional: a custom sending domain (paid plans). Default addresses are @agentmail.to.
# AGENTMAIL_DOMAIN=

# --- fig's own payment cards (AgentCard) ---
# Gives fig its OWN prepaid virtual Visa cards, so it can pay at checkout while browsing
# (mint a card for the exact amount, fill the form). Wired ONLY into the browse specialist;
# spending/closing/revealing a card all ask you first. Skipped while the token is blank.
#   1. npm install -g agent-cards  &&  agent-cards signup   (click the magic link in email)
#   2. Grab the token:  cat ~/.agent-cards/config.json   → copy the "jwt" value
#   3. Paste it below. New accounts start in TEST mode (sandbox cards); fig can switch to
#      prod (real cards) with your 👍 when you're ready.
AGENTCARD_TOKEN=

# --- fig's own phone number + verification codes (read_codes) ---
# Gives fig its OWN phone line for phone-number signups, and lets it read the SMS/iMessage
# verification codes that land on it via the read_codes tool. read_codes queries the local
# BlueBubbles server (the iMessage bridge the relay already runs on the Mac mini) read-only
# — BlueBubbles holds Full Disk Access and decodes message bodies, so no extra setup.
# IMPORTANT: keep this line separate from any number that receives your personal
# banking/2FA codes — read_codes can read anything that lands on it.
AGENT_PHONE_NUMBER=
BLUEBUBBLES_URL=http://localhost:1234
BLUEBUBBLES_PASSWORD=

# --- Order approval anchors (optional) ---
# What a "normal" order looks like for you, as JSON. The order gate never blocks or approves
# on this — it only annotates the 🔐 with what's off-pattern ("paying with VISA …4242, not the
# usual …1234"). Unset means the only anchor is the hard ceiling.
#   cardLast4/fulfillment: the card and pickup-vs-delivery you normally use
#   merchants: merchant-name substring → { storeId, storeLabel, typicalTotal, domain }
#   ceiling: a total above this is flagged no matter the merchant
#   driftFraction: how far off typicalTotal counts as "well off the usual"
# ORDER_APPROVAL_EXPECTATIONS={"cardLast4":"1234","fulfillment":"PICKUP","merchants":{"chipotle":{"storeId":"9900123","storeLabel":"12 Example Ave","typicalTotal":19.93,"domain":"chipotle.com"}},"ceiling":75,"driftFraction":0.4}
